+Two independent accounts hitting one identical post-build failure mode points at a **repo-level or platform-plane cause**, not an account quota, a service misconfiguration unique to one service, or a build artifact problem. The `updatedAt` values (10-24T08:32Z, config-era) bracket the same "last successful deploy" anchor the #2211 admin timeline already established. Build-minute counters (~42/500 ≈ three 14-minute builds this month) are consistent with a handful of push-triggered attempts since the last success — each new core commit burns ~14 build minutes and lands in the same update_failed state.
+
+## 7. Tower tooling surface: what the MCP plane can and cannot do here
+
+Facts the admin needs when choosing a recovery lane (capability advertisement vs implementation, verified this cycle):
+
+- `resources` registry advertises the render resources with capabilities `["health","logs","deploy","restart","env_vars"]`, but `render_manage` implements **only** `list_services | service_health | get_logs`. There is **no restart, no deploy trigger, and no env-var tool** behind `render_manage`.
+- A deploy trigger does exist as the separate policy-gated action tool `action_render_deploy` (`serviceId` required, optional `clearCache`, optional `overrideRequestId` for approved HITL requests). Without an approved HITL request it is subject to the policy engine — and per the "build logs first" doctrine a blind redeploy is contraindicated anyway; this receipt is the build-log review that would precede any such approval.
+- `render_get_logs` exposes `type=app|build` only (public HTTP request logs intentionally not exposed), `limit <= 100`, window-bounded. **Render's deploy events API (deploy history, per-deploy status transitions, instance-hours quota) is not reachable from the tower** — the update-phase failure detail lives behind that unreachable surface.
+
+Net: **the tower MCP plane cannot execute the recovery** (no restart action; deploy is HITL-gated) **and cannot see the failing phase's event log**. Recovery is admin-lane: Render dashboard → Deploys tab for `srv-dau7c9ek1f9s73almn0g` / `srv-dabm7evqj5pc738jkf30` → read the update_failed deploy's event detail (and the account instance-hours meter for the free plan), then choose manual redeploy / plan change / Render support. Build logs say the artifact is good; whatever kills the update phase will be visible in the deploy event detail the tower cannot fetch.
+
+## 8. Never-do (lane discipline)
+
+- Do **not** fire `action_render_deploy` without an approved HITL request and without this receipt's build-log review context — blind redeploys burn 14 build minutes each and the update phase has failed identically at least three times.
+- Do **not** revert core code: #2228 falsified the CI lead and this receipt exonerates the build; there is no code-level culprit to revert to.
+- Do **not** treat the duo's `degraded` board color as runtime risk: runtime is clean (§4); the exposure is staleness of the running build, not availability.
+- Do **not** re-file this evidence per sentry generation: reference #2225 + this receipt instead (zero-duplicate law).
